WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.480
- [Instructor] Earlier in
your algebraic careers,

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:04.840
you learned how to multiply polynomials.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:09.840
So for example, if we had x
plus two times four x plus five,

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:14.020
we learned that this is the same thing

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:16.410
as really doing the
distributive property twice.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:20.720
But you can multiply x times
four x to get four x-squared.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.940
You can multiply x times
five to get five x.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:27.430
You can multiply two times
four x to get eight x.

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:30.080
And you can multiply two
times five to get 10.

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:32.790
If what I just did looks foreign to you,

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:34.540
I encourage you to review

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:37.460
multiplying binomials on Kahn Academy.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:39.880
But then this could be simplified as being

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:44.880
four x-squared plus 13x plus 10.

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:48.810
What we will now do as we
advance our algebraic careers

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:50.990
is to think about how do
we go the other way around?

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:54.140
How do we divide polynomials?

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:57.320
So for example, we might
see an expression like this.

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:02.320
Four x-squared plus 13x plus
10 divided by x plus two.

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:08.470
Now, you might already
know from your knowledge

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:10.730
of multiplication and
division that this is really

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:12.840
reversing what we just did up here,

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:16.280
that if x plus two times four x plus five

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:19.980
is equal to this business,
then this business

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:24.093
divided by x plus two should
be equal to four x plus five.

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:27.290
And even if you didn't
have this information

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:28.420
that we have up here,

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:30.540
there's ways that you
would've approached this.

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:32.590
One way is that you
could've tried to factor

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:34.790
what we have here in the numerator.

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:38.600
You could've said hey, let me
maybe factor this by grouping.

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:39.780
And let's do that right over here.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:43.740
Four x-squared plus 13x plus 10.

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:45.440
When you factor by grouping, you say hey,

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:48.240
can I think of two numbers whose product

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:49.550
is equal to four times 10?

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:52.390
So I say a times b is equal to 40.

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:54.830
And whose sum is equal to 13.

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:58.330
So a plus b is equal to 13.

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:00.810
And let's see what it could be.

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:03.930
It could be eight and five.

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:06.700
So a is equal to eight,
b is equal to five.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:08.800
This is just factoring by
grouping right over here.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:10.780
Once again, if this is unfamiliar to you,

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:11.980
I encourage you to review

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:13.800
factoring by grouping on Kahn Academy.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.980
And so we can break apart this 13x

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:18.050
as an eight x and a five x.

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:21.060
So we can rewrite this as four x-squared

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:26.990
plus eight x plus five x plus 10, plus 10.

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:31.060
And notice, it's just re,
undoing what we did up here.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:32.350
But we're assuming we don't even know

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:35.080
about what we did up here,
that someone just gave us

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:37.260
this quadratic and said factor it.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:40.010
And then here you could say
all right, for these first

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:42.640
two terms, I could factor out a four x.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:45.430
So it becomes four x times x plus two.

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:48.640
And then these second two terms,
I could factor out a five.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:51.770
So plus five times x plus two.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:53.450
And then I can factor out an x plus two.

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:57.320
So it becomes, it becomes x plus two.

00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:59.260
I'll scroll down a little bit.

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:03.433
It become x plus two, I'm
just factoring that out,

00:03:03.433 --> 00:03:08.433
times four x plus five, four x plus five,

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:11.570
which is exactly what we had up there.

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:14.560
And so you could rewrite this expression

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:16.190
that involves division,

00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:19.060
sometimes called a
rational expression, as,

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:24.060
we could rewrite this as x plus
two times four x plus five,

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:29.710
all of that over x plus two.

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:33.600
And as long as x does
not equal negative two,

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:35.360
we can divide the
numerator and denominator

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:37.810
by x plus two and we're going to be left

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:40.500
with four x plus five.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:41.700
And we could constrain it.

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:46.700
We could say for x not
equaling negative two.

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:50.390
This is just a little bit of a primer.

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:53.700
As we go deeper, we'll do many,
many, many examples of this.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:55.920
And we'll also see that
there's other techniques

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:58.970
other than just factoring
this numerator over here.

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:01.060
And we're going to do it with
higher degree polynomials,

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:02.590
third degree polynomials.

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:05.960
And we're gonna learn something
known as polynomial division

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:07.540
where it's going to look an awful lot,

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:09.200
and it's actually gonna
have a lot of similarities,

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:11.680
with the long division
that you likely learned

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:14.130
in probably fourth or fifth grade,

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:17.010
where we're gonna take
expressions like x plus two

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:19.470
and we're gonna divide it into

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:23.730
four x-squared plus 13x plus 10.

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:26.370
And instead of place value,
we'll have a new notion

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:28.100
of place value which
is around which degree

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:29.110
term you're thinking about.

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:31.330
So we're gonna do things
like, and we're gonna do these

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:33.840
completely in other
videos, hey, how many times

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.430
does x go into four x-squared?

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:37.790
Hey, it goes four x times.

00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:39.230
So you'd write the four x there

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:41.160
and then you'd multiply
four x times x-squared.

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:42.610
You get four x-squared.

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:44.800
Four x times two is eight x.

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:46.350
Then you subtract these.

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:48.480
And then you keep going
just like you would

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:49.720
typically do long division.

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:52.030
13x minus eight x is five x.

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:54.260
And then you bring that 10 down.

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:55.740
And then you say hey, how many times

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:56.617
does x go into five x?

00:04:56.617 --> 00:04:59.470
And you say hey, it goes five times.

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:04.020
Five times x is equal to five x.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:06.560
Five times two is equal to 10.

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:09.030
And then you subtract this

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:11.260
and you're left with no remainder.

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:13.040
And so notice, you just
saw when x plus two

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:16.310
is divided into this,
you get four x plus five,

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:18.270
just what we saw right over there.

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:20.150
So we're gonna explore
these multiple techniques,

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:21.560
including polynomial division.

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:23.540
And we're also going to see what happens

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:25.833
when you do have a remainder there.

